{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09658","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09658","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09658","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingkui","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Bingkui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaer","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Jiaer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sifeng","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Sifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Kaiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Measuring Epistemic Humility in Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:54:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:49:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-25T08:56:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:36:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T07:55:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-15T00:52:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hallucinations in multimodal large language models (MLLMs), where a model generates content inconsistent with the input image, pose significant risks in real-world applications. Existing hallucination benchmarks primarily test recognition, i.e., whether a model can select the correct answer among distractors. They overlook another capability that is essential for trustworthy AI: recognizing when none of the provided options is supported by the image and declining to commit to a false choice. We present HumbleBench, a hallucination benchmark that evaluates this false-option rejection behavior, a directly observable facet of epistemic humility, through multiple-choice questions that always include a \"None of the above\" (NOTA) option. HumbleBench is built from the Panoptic Scene Graph dataset: its object and relation annotations, together with candidate attribute cues, are used to prompt GPT-4-Turbo to generate questions, which are then manually verified, yielding 22,831 questions on object, relation, and attribute hallucinations. We evaluate 27 MLLMs, including general-purpose, reasoning-oriented, and proprietary models, and complement standard accuracy with NOTA-aware metrics, diagnostic stress tests, and a human evaluation. Even the strongest models select NOTA for only about half of the questions in which it is correct, cautious prompting shifts models toward NOTA without improving their ability to detect unsupported options, and humans reject unsupported options considerably more reliably than all evaluated models. These results show that recognition accuracy alone is insufficient for assessing hallucination robustness. Our code and dataset are publicly available at https://github.com/maifoundations/HumbleBench."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09658","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:39:07Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:39:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-15T03:09:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Illia","familyName":"Volkov","name":"Volkov, Illia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Kisel","name":"Kisel, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Klara","familyName":"Janouskova","name":"Janouskova, Klara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiri","familyName":"Matas","name":"Matas, Jiri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Image Recognition with Vision and Language Embeddings of VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:54:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:27:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T09:13:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:51:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) have enabled strong zero-shot classification through image-text alignment. Yet, their purely visual inference capabilities remain under-explored. In this work, we conduct a comprehensive evaluation of both language-guided and vision-only image classification with a diverse set of dual-encoder VLMs, including both well-established and recent models such as SigLIP 2 and RADIOv2.5. The performance is compared in a standard setup on the ImageNet-1k validation set and its label-corrected variant. The key factors affecting accuracy are analysed, including prompt design, class diversity, the number of neighbours in k-NN, and reference set size. We show that language and vision offer complementary strengths, with some classes favouring textual prompts and others better handled by visual similarity. To exploit this complementarity, we introduce a simple, learning-free fusion method based on per-class precision that improves classification performance. The code is available at: https://github.com/gonikisgo/bmvc2025-vlm-image-recognition."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:59Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:31:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:14:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08897","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08897","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08897","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Caffagni","name":"Caffagni, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Sarto","name":"Sarto, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcella","familyName":"Cornia","name":"Cornia, Marcella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Baraldi","name":"Baraldi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rita","familyName":"Cucchiara","name":"Cucchiara, Rita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Recurrence Meets Transformers for Universal Multimodal Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:00:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:01:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T08:11:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:32:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T12:08:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:49:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of multimodal retrieval and its application in LLMs and multimodal LLMs, increasingly complex retrieval tasks have emerged. Existing methods predominantly rely on task-specific fine-tuning of vision-language models and are limited to single-modality queries or documents. In this paper, we propose ReT-2, a unified retrieval model that supports multimodal queries, composed of both images and text, and searches across multimodal document collections where text and images coexist. ReT-2 leverages multi-layer representations and a recurrent Transformer architecture with LSTM-inspired gating mechanisms to dynamically integrate information across layers and modalities, capturing fine-grained visual and textual details. We evaluate ReT-2 on the challenging M2KR and M-BEIR benchmarks across different retrieval configurations. Results demonstrate that ReT-2 consistently achieves state-of-the-art performance across diverse settings, while offering faster inference and reduced memory usage compared to prior approaches. When integrated into retrieval-augmented generation pipelines, ReT-2 also improves downstream performance on Encyclopedic-VQA and InfoSeek datasets. Our source code and trained models are publicly available at: https://github.com/aimagelab/ReT-2"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"TPAMI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08897","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:21:10Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:21:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:40:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09151","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09151","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09151","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syuan-Hao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Syuan-Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piotr","familyName":"Koniusz","name":"Koniusz, Piotr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongsheng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Video Understanding by Design: How Datasets Shape Video Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T05:06:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:16:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T07:16:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:26:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Research in video understanding has advanced rapidly, driven by increasingly diverse datasets and more powerful model architectures. While existing surveys typically organize progress by tasks, benchmarks, or model families, they provide limited insight into why particular architectures emerged and succeeded. In this survey, we argue that the evolution of video understanding is fundamentally shaped by dataset structure. We present a dataset-centric perspective that connects dataset structure, inductive biases, and architectural design within a unified framework. We show that different datasets require models to capture specific invariances and capabilities, such as robustness to viewpoint changes, sensitivity to temporal ordering, reasoning over long-range dependencies, relational interactions, and cross-modal alignment. These requirements naturally give rise to inductive biases, i.e., architectural assumptions that favor particular patterns of reasoning and generalization. From this perspective, milestone architectures, including two-stream networks, 3D CNNs, temporal models, transformers, graph-based methods, and multimodal foundation models, can be understood as architectural responses to the challenges posed by evolving datasets. Building on this framework, we systematically analyze how dataset characteristics have shaped architectural innovation across video understanding tasks and discuss the representational biases induced by different data regimes. By unifying datasets, inductive biases, and architectures into a coherent perspective, this survey offers both a retrospective explanation of the field's evolution and a forward-looking roadmap toward general-purpose video understanding systems. Code and dynamic video visualizations of dataset-induced biases are available at https://time.griffith.edu.au/paper-sites/video-understanding/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Research report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09151","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:07Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:12:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09530","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09530","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09530","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul F. R.","familyName":"Wilson","name":"Wilson, Paul F. R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Ronchetti","name":"Ronchetti, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rüdiger","familyName":"Göbl","name":"Göbl, Rüdiger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viktoria","familyName":"Markova","name":"Markova, Viktoria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Rosenzweig","name":"Rosenzweig, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Prevost","name":"Prevost, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parvin","familyName":"Mousavi","name":"Mousavi, Parvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Zettinig","name":"Zettinig, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DualTrack: Sensorless 3D Ultrasound needs Local and Global Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T15:13:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:41:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-17T13:31:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:49:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Three-dimensional ultrasound (US) offers many clinical advantages over conventional 2D imaging, yet its widespread adoption is limited by the cost and complexity of traditional 3D systems. Sensorless 3D US, which uses deep learning to estimate a 3D probe trajectory from a sequence of 2D US images, is a promising alternative. Local features, such as speckle patterns, can help predict frame-to-frame motion, while global features, such as coarse shapes and anatomical structures, can situate the scan relative to anatomy and help predict its general shape. In prior approaches, global features are either ignored or tightly coupled with local feature extraction, restricting the ability to robustly model these two complementary aspects. We propose DualTrack, a novel dual-encoder architecture that leverages decoupled local and global encoders specialized for their respective scales of feature extraction. The local encoder uses dense spatiotemporal convolutions to capture fine-grained features, while the global encoder utilizes an image backbone (e.g., a 2D CNN or foundation model) and temporal attention layers to embed high-level anatomical features and long-range dependencies. A lightweight fusion module then combines these features to estimate the trajectory. Experimental results on a large public benchmark show that DualTrack achieves state-of-the-art accuracy and globally consistent 3D reconstructions, outperforming previous methods and yielding an average reconstruction error below 5 mm."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09530","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:36:08Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:36:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:08:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09666","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09666","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09666","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiqing","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Kaiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongjian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zongjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyan","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Junyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haochen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhendong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Xinyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingdong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haifeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unified Multimodal Models as Auto-Encoders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:57:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:49:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T20:35:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:11:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T09:54:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:39:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-26T09:05:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:34:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-31T09:23:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:42:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Image-to-text (I2T) understanding and text-to-image (T2I) generation are two fundamental, important yet traditionally isolated multimodal tasks. Despite their intrinsic connection, existing approaches typically optimize them independently, missing the opportunity for mutual enhancement. In this paper, we argue that the both tasks can be connected under a shared Auto-Encoder perspective, where text serves as the intermediate latent representation bridging the two directions - encoding images into textual semantics (I2T) and decoding text back into images (T2I). Our key insight is that if the encoder truly \"understands\" the image, it should capture all essential structure, and if the decoder truly \"understands\" the text, it should recover that structure faithfully. Building upon this principle, we propose Unified-GRPO, a post-training method based on reinforcement learning that jointly optimizes both modules through reconstructive rewards, maximizing the semantic consistency between the input and the generated images. Under this reconstruction objective, the encoder is encouraged to extract as much accurate and comprehensive semantic information from the input image to maximize reconstruction quality, while the decoder is simultaneously optimized to generate conditioned on the encoder's prior, enabling a self-evolving improvement. Empirically, we find that using text as the intermediate representation and training under a reconstructive RL paradigm effectively benefits both I2T and T2I. The I2T module gains stronger fine-grained visual perception, such as small-object recognition, grounding, etc, while its dense embeddings and language priors, in turn, provide richer semantic signals that improve T2I fidelity and complex instruction following. These results demonstrate that the reconstructive RL establishes a mutually reinforcing cross-modal synergy within the auto-encoding framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09666","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:39:19Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:39:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:30:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09509","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09509","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09509","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro Miguel Bastos","familyName":"Soares","name":"Soares, Pedro Miguel Bastos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Tourani","name":"Tourani, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Fernandez-Cortizas","name":"Fernandez-Cortizas, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asier","familyName":"Bikandi-Noya","name":"Bikandi-Noya, Asier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Holger","familyName":"Voos","name":"Voos, Holger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose Luis","familyName":"Sanchez-Lopez","name":"Sanchez-Lopez, Jose Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SMapper: A Multi-Modal Data Acquisition Platform for SLAM Benchmarking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T14:50:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:40:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T14:32:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T02:53:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s10846-026-02351-7","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advancing research in fields such as Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) and autonomous navigation critically depends on the availability of reliable and reproducible multimodal datasets. While several influential datasets have driven progress in these domains, they often suffer from limitations in sensing modalities, environmental diversity, and the reproducibility of the underlying hardware setups. To address these challenges, this paper introduces SMapper, a novel open-hardware, multi-sensor platform designed explicitly for, though not limited to, SLAM research. The device integrates synchronized LiDAR, multi-camera, and inertial sensing, supported by a robust calibration and synchronization pipeline that ensures precise spatio-temporal alignment across modalities. Its open and replicable design allows researchers to extend its capabilities and reproduce experiments across both handheld and robot-mounted scenarios. To demonstrate its practicality, we additionally release SMapper-light, a publicly available SLAM dataset containing representative indoor and outdoor sequences. The dataset includes tightly synchronized multimodal data and ground truth trajectories derived from offline LiDAR-based SLAM with sub-centimeter accuracy, alongside dense 3D reconstructions. Furthermore, the paper contains benchmarking results on state-of-the-art LiDAR and visual SLAM frameworks using the SMapper-light dataset. By combining open-hardware design, reproducible data collection, and comprehensive benchmarking, SMapper establishes a robust foundation for advancing SLAM algorithm development, evaluation, and reproducibility. The project's documentation, including source code, CAD models, and dataset links, is publicly available at https://snt-arg.github.io/smapper_docs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 5 figures, 6 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09509","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-12T01:35:39Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:35:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:38:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09332","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09332","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09332","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuecheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dafeng","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Dafeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiguang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanguang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhanguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzheng","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Yuzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengwei","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Pengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Gamaliel Arcos","familyName":"Bravo","name":"Bravo, David Gamaliel Arcos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingxue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yingxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianye","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Jianye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyue","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Xingyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OmniEVA: Embodied Versatile Planner via Task-Adaptive 3D-Grounded and Embodiment-aware Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T10:32:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:28:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T08:01:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:22:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T05:17:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-29T01:23:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have opened new opportunities for embodied intelligence, enabling multimodal understanding, reasoning, and interaction, as well as continuous spatial decision-making. Nevertheless, current MLLM-based embodied systems face two critical limitations. First, Geometric Adaptability Gap: models trained solely on 2D inputs or with hard-coded 3D geometry injection suffer from either insufficient spatial information or restricted 2D generalization, leading to poor adaptability across tasks with diverse spatial demands. Second, Embodiment Constraint Gap: prior work often neglects the physical constraints and capacities of real robots, resulting in task plans that are theoretically valid but practically infeasible. To address these gaps, we introduce OmniEVA -- an embodied versatile planner that enables advanced embodied reasoning and task planning through two pivotal innovations: (1) a Task-Adaptive 3D Grounding mechanism, which introduces a gated router to perform explicit selective regulation of 3D fusion based on contextual requirements, enabling context-aware 3D grounding for diverse embodied tasks. (2) an Embodiment-Aware Reasoning framework that jointly incorporates task goals and embodiment constraints into the reasoning loop, resulting in planning decisions that are both goal-directed and executable. Extensive experimental results demonstrate that OmniEVA not only achieves state-of-the-art general embodied reasoning performance, but also exhibits a strong ability across a wide range of downstream scenarios. Evaluations of a suite of proposed embodied benchmarks, including both primitive and composite tasks, confirm its robust and versatile planning capabilities. Project page: https://omnieva.github.io"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09332","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:31:28Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:31:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-29T03:12:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09116","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09116","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09116","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryohei","familyName":"Miyakawa","name":"Miyakawa, Ryohei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinpeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Risa","familyName":"Shinoda","name":"Shinoda, Risa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroaki","familyName":"Santo","name":"Santo, Hiroaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yosuke","familyName":"Toda","name":"Toda, Yosuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fumio","familyName":"Okura","name":"Okura, Fumio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zero-shot Hierarchical Plant Segmentation via Foundation Segmentation Models and Text-to-image Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T02:53:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:12:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T10:47:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:39:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T05:20:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-22T01:18:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation segmentation models achieve reasonable leaf instance extraction from top-view crop images without training (i.e., zero-shot). However, segmenting entire plant individuals with each consisting of multiple overlapping leaves remains challenging. This problem is referred to as a hierarchical segmentation task, typically requiring annotated training datasets, which are often species-specific and require notable human labor. To address this, we introduce ZeroPlantSeg, a zero-shot segmentation for rosette-shaped plant individuals from top-view images. We integrate a foundation segmentation model, extracting leaf instances, and a vision-language model, reasoning about plants' structures to extract plant individuals without additional training. Evaluations on datasets with multiple plant species, growth stages, and shooting environments demonstrate that our method surpasses existing zero-shot methods and achieves better cross-domain performance than supervised methods. Implementations are available at https://github.com/JunhaoXing/ZeroPlantSeg."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"WACV 2026 Accepted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09116","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:18Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-22T03:03:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09163","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09163","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09163","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulin","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Yulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengzong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fengzong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiqin","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Haiqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CWSSNet: Hyperspectral Image Classification Enhanced by Wavelet Domain Convolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T05:44:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:17:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T09:18:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:33:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hyperspectral remote sensing technology has significant application value in fields such as forestry ecology and precision agriculture, while also putting forward higher requirements for fine ground object classification. However, although hyperspectral images are rich in spectral information and can improve recognition accuracy, they tend to cause prominent feature redundancy due to their numerous bands, high dimensionality, and spectral mixing characteristics. To address this, this study used hyperspectral images from the ZY1F satellite as a data source and selected Yugan County, Shangrao City, Jiangxi Province as the research area to perform ground object classification research. A classification framework named CWSSNet was proposed, which integrates 3D spectral-spatial features and wavelet convolution. This framework integrates multimodal information us-ing a multiscale convolutional attention module and breaks through the classification performance bottleneck of traditional methods by introducing multi-band decomposition and convolution operations in the wavelet domain. The experiments showed that CWSSNet achieved 74.50\\%, 82.73\\%, and 84.94\\% in mean Intersection over Union (mIoU), mean Accuracy (mAcc), and mean F1-score (mF1) respectively in Yugan County. It also obtained the highest Intersection over Union (IoU) in the classifica-tion of water bodies, vegetation, and bare land, demonstrating good robustness. Additionally, when the training set proportion was 70\\%, the increase in training time was limited, and the classification effect was close to the optimal level, indicating that the model maintains reliable performance under small-sample training conditions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09163","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:25Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-05T03:11:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geonsun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Geonsun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nels","familyName":"Numan","name":"Numan, Nels","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhe","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Achin","familyName":"Kulshrestha","name":"Kulshrestha, Achin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ishan","familyName":"Chatterjee","name":"Chatterjee, Ishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinda","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yinda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dinesh","familyName":"Manocha","name":"Manocha, Dinesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruofei","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Ruofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sensible Agent: A Framework for Unobtrusive Interaction with Proactive AR Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:40:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:33:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T19:39:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:07:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746059.3747748","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Proactive AR agents promise context-aware assistance, but their interactions often rely on explicit voice prompts or responses, which can be disruptive or socially awkward. We introduce Sensible Agent, a framework designed for unobtrusive interaction with these proactive agents. Sensible Agent dynamically adapts both \"what\" assistance to offer and, crucially, \"how\" to deliver it, based on real-time multimodal context sensing. Informed by an expert workshop (n=12) and a data annotation study (n=40), the framework leverages egocentric cameras, multimodal sensing, and Large Multimodal Models (LMMs) to infer context and suggest appropriate actions delivered via minimally intrusive interaction modes. We demonstrate our prototype on an XR headset through a user study (n=10) in both AR and VR scenarios. Results indicate that Sensible Agent significantly reduces perceived interaction effort compared to voice-prompted baseline, while maintaining high usability and achieving higher preference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:37Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-03T03:00:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09372","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09372","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09372","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxiang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Pengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyang","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Xinyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxu","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Pengxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siteng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Siteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ru","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VLA-Adapter: An Effective Paradigm for Tiny-Scale Vision-Language-Action Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T11:42:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:31:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T17:22:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:49:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models typically bridge the gap between perceptual and action spaces by pre-training a large-scale Vision-Language Model (VLM) on robotic data. While this approach greatly enhances performance, it also incurs significant training costs. In this paper, we investigate how to effectively bridge vision-language (VL) representations to action (A). We introduce VLA-Adapter, a novel paradigm designed to reduce the reliance of VLA models on large-scale VLMs and extensive pre-training. To this end, we first systematically analyze the effectiveness of various VL conditions and present key findings on which conditions are essential for bridging perception and action spaces. Based on these insights, we propose a lightweight Policy module with Bridge Attention, which autonomously injects the optimal condition into the action space. In this way, our method achieves high performance using only a 0.5B-parameter backbone, without any robotic data pre-training. Extensive experiments on both simulated and real-world robotic benchmarks demonstrate that VLA-Adapter not only achieves state-of-the-art level performance, but also offers the fast inference speed reported to date. Furthermore, thanks to the proposed advanced bridging paradigm, VLA-Adapter enables the training of a powerful VLA model in just 8 hours on a single consumer-grade GPU, greatly lowering the barrier to deploying the VLA model. Project page: https://vla-adapter.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages; Project page: https://vla-adapter.github.io/; Github: https://github.com/OpenHelix-Team/VLA-Adapter; HuggingFace: https://huggingface.co/VLA-Adapter"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09372","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:32:25Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:32:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:55:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09595","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09595","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09595","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zekun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyuan","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Liyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingming","familyName":"Lao","name":"Lao, Mingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingchao","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Yingchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Shen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu-Shen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kling-Avatar: Grounding Multimodal Instructions for Cascaded Long-Duration Avatar Animation Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:34:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:46:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T04:08:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:20:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in audio-driven avatar video generation have significantly enhanced audio-visual realism. However, existing methods treat instruction conditioning merely as low-level tracking driven by acoustic or visual cues, without modeling the communicative purpose conveyed by the instructions. This limitation compromises their narrative coherence and character expressiveness. To bridge this gap, we introduce Kling-Avatar, a novel cascaded framework that unifies multimodal instruction understanding with photorealistic portrait generation. Our approach adopts a two-stage pipeline. In the first stage, we design a multimodal large language model (MLLM) director that produces a blueprint video conditioned on diverse instruction signals, thereby governing high-level semantics such as character motion and emotions. In the second stage, guided by blueprint keyframes, we generate multiple sub-clips in parallel using a first-last frame strategy. This global-to-local framework preserves fine-grained details while faithfully encoding the high-level intent behind multimodal instructions. Our parallel architecture also enables fast and stable generation of long-duration videos, making it suitable for real-world applications such as digital human livestreaming and vlogging. To comprehensively evaluate our method, we construct a benchmark of 375 curated samples covering diverse instructions and challenging scenarios. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Kling-Avatar is capable of generating vivid, fluent, long-duration videos at up to 1080p and 48 fps, achieving superior performance in lip synchronization accuracy, emotion and dynamic expressiveness, instruction controllability, identity preservation, and cross-domain generalization. These results establish Kling-Avatar as a new benchmark for semantically grounded, high-fidelity audio-driven avatar synthesis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical Report. Project Page: https://klingavatar.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09595","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:37:39Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:37:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:44:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09130","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09130","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09130","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingxuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huafeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huafeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiegen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qiegen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ALL-PET: A Low-resource and Low-shot PET Foundation Model in Projection Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T04:03:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:14:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T09:11:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:34:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Building large-scale foundation model for PET imaging is hindered by limited access to labeled data and insufficient computational resources. To overcome data scarcity and efficiency limitations, we propose ALL-PET, a low-resource, low-shot PET foundation model operating directly in projection domain. ALL-PET leverages a latent diffusion model (LDM) with three key innovations. First, we design a Radon mask augmentation strategy (RMAS) that generates over 200,000 structurally diverse training samples by projecting randomized image-domain masks into sinogram space, significantly improving generalization with minimal data. This is extended by a dynamic multi-mask (DMM) mechanism that varies mask quantity and distribution, enhancing data diversity without added model complexity. Second, we implement positive/negative mask constraints to embed strict geometric consistency, reducing parameter burden while preserving generation quality. Third, we introduce transparent medical attention (TMA), a parameter-free, geometry-driven mechanism that enhances lesion-related regions in raw projection data. Lesion-focused attention maps are derived from coarse segmentation, covering both hypermetabolic and hypometabolic areas, and projected into sinogram space for physically consistent guidance. The system supports clinician-defined ROI adjustments, ensuring flexible, interpretable, and task-adaptive emphasis aligned with PET acquisition physics. Experimental results show that ALL-PET achieves high-quality sinogram generation using only 500 samples, with performance comparable to models trained on larger datasets. ALL-PET generalizes across tasks including low-dose reconstruction, attenuation correction, delayed-frame prediction, and tracer separation, operating efficiently with memory use under 24GB."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09130","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:37Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:11:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09680","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09680","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09680","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongyao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Rongyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aldrich","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Aldrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengqi","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Chengqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linjiang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Linjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Si","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Si","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xihui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongsheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FLUX-Reason-6M \u0026amp; PRISM-Bench: A Million-Scale Text-to-Image Reasoning Dataset and Comprehensive Benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:59:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:49:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The advancement of open-source text-to-image (T2I) models has been hindered by the absence of large-scale, reasoning-focused datasets and comprehensive evaluation benchmarks, resulting in a performance gap compared to leading closed-source systems. To address this challenge, We introduce FLUX-Reason-6M and PRISM-Bench (Precise and Robust Image Synthesis Measurement Benchmark). FLUX-Reason-6M is a massive dataset consisting of 6 million high-quality FLUX-generated images and 20 million bilingual (English and Chinese) descriptions specifically designed to teach complex reasoning. The image are organized according to six key characteristics: Imagination, Entity, Text rendering, Style, Affection, and Composition, and design explicit Generation Chain-of-Thought (GCoT) to provide detailed breakdowns of image generation steps. The whole data curation takes 15,000 A100 GPU days, providing the community with a resource previously unattainable outside of large industrial labs. PRISM-Bench offers a novel evaluation standard with seven distinct tracks, including a formidable Long Text challenge using GCoT. Through carefully designed prompts, it utilizes advanced vision-language models for nuanced human-aligned assessment of prompt-image alignment and image aesthetics. Our extensive evaluation of 19 leading models on PRISM-Bench reveals critical performance gaps and highlights specific areas requiring improvement. Our dataset, benchmark, and evaluation code are released to catalyze the next wave of reasoning-oriented T2I generation. Project page: https://flux-reason-6m.github.io/ ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://flux-reason-6m.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09680","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:39:39Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:39:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:39:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09674","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09674","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09674","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haozhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiale","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jiale","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuekai","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xuekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianxing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tianxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ganqu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Ganqu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingxiang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Dingxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youbang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Youbang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangmiao","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Jiangmiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanghang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shanghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SimpleVLA-RL: Scaling VLA Training via Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:59:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:49:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have recently emerged as a powerful paradigm for robotic manipulation. Despite substantial progress enabled by large-scale pretraining and supervised fine-tuning (SFT), these models face two fundamental challenges: (i) the scarcity and high cost of large-scale human-operated robotic trajectories required for SFT scaling, and (ii) limited generalization to tasks involving distribution shift. Recent breakthroughs in Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) demonstrate that reinforcement learning (RL) can dramatically enhance step-by-step reasoning capabilities, raising a natural question: Can RL similarly improve the long-horizon step-by-step action planning of VLA? In this work, we introduce SimpleVLA-RL, an efficient RL framework tailored for VLA models. Building upon veRL, we introduce VLA-specific trajectory sampling, scalable parallelization, multi-environment rendering, and optimized loss computation. When applied to OpenVLA-OFT, SimpleVLA-RL achieves SoTA performance on LIBERO and even outperforms $π_0$ on RoboTwin 1.0\\\u0026amp;2.0 with the exploration-enhancing strategies we introduce. SimpleVLA-RL not only reduces dependence on large-scale data and enables robust generalization, but also remarkably surpasses SFT in real-world tasks. Moreover, we identify a novel phenomenon ``pushcut'' during RL training, wherein the policy discovers previously unseen patterns beyond those seen in the previous training process. Github: https://github.com/PRIME-RL/SimpleVLA-RL"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09674","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:39:30Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:39:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:39:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09584","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09584","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09584","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingdong","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Lingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyue","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Dongyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshuai","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Tianshuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lai Xing","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Lai Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei Tsang","familyName":"Ooi","name":"Ooi, Wei Tsang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit R.","familyName":"Cottereau","name":"Cottereau, Benoit R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Grounding from Event Cameras"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:21:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:45:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Event cameras capture changes in brightness with microsecond precision and remain reliable under motion blur and challenging illumination, offering clear advantages for modeling highly dynamic scenes. Yet, their integration with natural language understanding has received little attention, leaving a gap in multimodal perception. To address this, we introduce Talk2Event, the first large-scale benchmark for language-driven object grounding using event data. Built on real-world driving scenarios, Talk2Event comprises 5,567 scenes, 13,458 annotated objects, and more than 30,000 carefully validated referring expressions. Each expression is enriched with four structured attributes -- appearance, status, relation to the viewer, and relation to surrounding objects -- that explicitly capture spatial, temporal, and relational cues. This attribute-centric design supports interpretable and compositional grounding, enabling analysis that moves beyond simple object recognition to contextual reasoning in dynamic environments. We envision Talk2Event as a foundation for advancing multimodal and temporally-aware perception, with applications spanning robotics, human-AI interaction, and so on."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Abstract Paper (Non-Archival) @ ICCV 2025 NeVi Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09584","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:37:23Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:37:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:37:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09572","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09572","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09572","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijun","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Sijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"LiBo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, LiBo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Geng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoliang","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Xiaoliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PeftCD: Leveraging Vision Foundation Models with Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning for Remote Sensing Change Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:08:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:44:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To tackle the prevalence of pseudo changes, the scarcity of labeled samples, and the difficulty of cross-domain generalization in multi-temporal and multi-source remote sensing imagery, we propose PeftCD, a change detection framework built upon Vision Foundation Models (VFMs) with Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT). At its core, PeftCD employs a weight-sharing Siamese encoder derived from a VFM, into which LoRA and Adapter modules are seamlessly integrated. This design enables highly efficient task adaptation by training only a minimal set of additional parameters. To fully unlock the potential of VFMs, we investigate two leading backbones: the Segment Anything Model v2 (SAM2), renowned for its strong segmentation priors, and DINOv3, a state-of-the-art self-supervised representation learner. The framework is complemented by a deliberately lightweight decoder, ensuring the focus remains on the powerful feature representations from the backbones. Extensive experiments demonstrate that PeftCD achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple public datasets, including SYSU-CD (IoU 73.81%), WHUCD (92.05%), MSRSCD (64.07%), MLCD (76.89%), CDD (97.01%), S2Looking (52.25%) and LEVIR-CD (85.62%), with notably precise boundary delineation and strong suppression of pseudo-changes. In summary, PeftCD presents an optimal balance of accuracy, efficiency, and generalization. It offers a powerful and scalable paradigm for adapting large-scale VFMs to real-world remote sensing change detection applications. The code and pretrained models will be released at https://github.com/dyzy41/PeftCD."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09572","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:37:07Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:37:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:37:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09541","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09541","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09541","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hala","familyName":"Hawashin","name":"Hawashin, Hala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mina","familyName":"Abbaszadeh","name":"Abbaszadeh, Mina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Joseph","name":"Joseph, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beth","familyName":"Pearson","name":"Pearson, Beth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martha","familyName":"Lewis","name":"Lewis, Martha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehrnoosh","familyName":"sadrzadeh","name":"sadrzadeh, Mehrnoosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compositional Concept Generalization with Variational Quantum Circuits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T15:34:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:42:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Compositional generalization is a key facet of human cognition, but lacking in current AI tools such as vision-language models. Previous work examined whether a compositional tensor-based sentence semantics can overcome the challenge, but led to negative results. We conjecture that the increased training efficiency of quantum models will improve performance in these tasks. We interpret the representations of compositional tensor-based models in Hilbert spaces and train Variational Quantum Circuits to learn these representations on an image captioning task requiring compositional generalization. We used two image encoding techniques: a multi-hot encoding (MHE) on binary image vectors and an angle/amplitude encoding on image vectors taken from the vision-language model CLIP. We achieve good proof-of-concept results using noisy MHE encodings. Performance on CLIP image vectors was more mixed, but still outperformed classical compositional models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to: 2025 IEEE International Conference on Quantum Artificial Intelligence (QAI), Naples, Italy, Nov 2-5, 2025. This is the authors' accepted manuscript (AAM). An IEEE copyright notice appears on page 1. The final published version will appear in IEEE Xplore; DOI to be added when available"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09541","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09473","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09473","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09473","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucie","familyName":"Poláková","name":"Poláková, Lucie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Popel","name":"Popel, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Věra","familyName":"Kloudová","name":"Kloudová, Věra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michal","familyName":"Novák","name":"Novák, Michal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mariia","familyName":"Anisimova","name":"Anisimova, Mariia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiří","familyName":"Balhar","name":"Balhar, Jiří","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating Language Barriers in Education: Developing Multilingual Digital Learning Materials with Machine Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T13:54:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:37:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.21125/edulearn.2025.2268","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The EdUKate project combines digital education, linguistics, translation studies, and machine translation to develop multilingual learning materials for Czech primary and secondary schools. Launched through collaboration between a major Czech academic institution and the country's largest educational publisher, the project is aimed at translating up to 9,000 multimodal interactive exercises from Czech into Ukrainian, English, and German for an educational web portal. It emphasizes the development and evaluation of a direct Czech-Ukrainian machine translation system tailored to the educational domain, with special attention to processing formatted content such as XML and PDF and handling technical and scientific terminology. We present findings from an initial survey of Czech teachers regarding the needs of non-Czech-speaking students and describe the system's evaluation and implementation on the web portal. All resulting applications are freely available to students, educators, and researchers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09473","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-12T01:34:48Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:34:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:34:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09427","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09427","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09427","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchan","familyName":"Jie","name":"Jie, Yuchan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yushen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaosong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuqiang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Fuqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianming","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Jianming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huafeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Huafeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FS-Diff: Semantic guidance and clarity-aware simultaneous multimodal image fusion and super-resolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T13:10:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:34:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.inffus.2025.103146","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As an influential information fusion and low-level vision technique, image fusion integrates complementary information from source images to yield an informative fused image. A few attempts have been made in recent years to jointly realize image fusion and super-resolution. However, in real-world applications such as military reconnaissance and long-range detection missions, the target and background structures in multimodal images are easily corrupted, with low resolution and weak semantic information, which leads to suboptimal results in current fusion techniques. In response, we propose FS-Diff, a semantic guidance and clarity-aware joint image fusion and super-resolution method. FS-Diff unifies image fusion and super-resolution as a conditional generation problem. It leverages semantic guidance from the proposed clarity sensing mechanism for adaptive low-resolution perception and cross-modal feature extraction. Specifically, we initialize the desired fused result as pure Gaussian noise and introduce the bidirectional feature Mamba to extract the global features of the multimodal images. Moreover, utilizing the source images and semantics as conditions, we implement a random iterative denoising process via a modified U-Net network. This network istrained for denoising at multiple noise levels to produce high-resolution fusion results with cross-modal features and abundant semantic information. We also construct a powerful aerial view multiscene (AVMS) benchmark covering 600 pairs of images. Extensive joint image fusion and super-resolution experiments on six public and our AVMS datasets demonstrated that FS-Diff outperforms the state-of-the-art methods at multiple magnifications and can recover richer details and semantics in the fused images. The code is available at https://github.com/XylonXu01/FS-Diff."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09427","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-12T01:33:43Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:33:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:33:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09356","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09356","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09356","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdel Hakim","familyName":"Drid","name":"Drid, Abdel Hakim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincenzo","familyName":"Suriani","name":"Suriani, Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniele","familyName":"Nardi","name":"Nardi, Daniele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abderrezzak","familyName":"Debilou","name":"Debilou, Abderrezzak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Curriculum-Based Multi-Tier Semantic Exploration via Deep Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T11:10:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:29:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Navigating and understanding complex and unknown environments autonomously demands more than just basic perception and movement from embodied agents. Truly effective exploration requires agents to possess higher-level cognitive abilities, the ability to reason about their surroundings, and make more informed decisions regarding exploration strategies. However, traditional RL approaches struggle to balance efficient exploration and semantic understanding due to limited cognitive capabilities embedded in the small policies for the agents, leading often to human drivers when dealing with semantic exploration. In this paper, we address this challenge by presenting a novel Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) architecture that is specifically designed for resource efficient semantic exploration. A key methodological contribution is the integration of a Vision-Language Model (VLM) common-sense through a layered reward function. The VLM query is modeled as a dedicated action, allowing the agent to strategically query the VLM only when deemed necessary for gaining external guidance, thereby conserving resources. This mechanism is combined with a curriculum learning strategy designed to guide learning at different levels of complexity to ensure robust and stable learning. Our experimental evaluation results convincingly demonstrate that our agent achieves significantly enhanced object discovery rates and develops a learned capability to effectively navigate towards semantically rich regions. Furthermore, it also shows a strategic mastery of when to prompt for external environmental information. By demonstrating a practical and scalable method for embedding common-sense semantic reasoning with autonomous agents, this research provides a novel approach to pursuing a fully intelligent and self-guided exploration in robotics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The 19th International Conference on Intelligent Autonomous Systems (IAS 19), 2025, Genoa"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09356","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:32:02Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:32:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:32:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09324","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09324","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09324","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"You","name":"You, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiqi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George Q.","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, George Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-Grained Customized Fashion Design with Image-into-Prompt benchmark and dataset from LMM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T10:14:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:27:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative AI evolves the execution of complex workflows in industry, where the large multimodal model empowers fashion design in the garment industry. Current generation AI models magically transform brainstorming into fancy designs easily, but the fine-grained customization still suffers from text uncertainty without professional background knowledge from end-users. Thus, we propose the Better Understanding Generation (BUG) workflow with LMM to automatically create and fine-grain customize the cloth designs from chat with image-into-prompt. Our framework unleashes users' creative potential beyond words and also lowers the barriers of clothing design/editing without further human involvement. To prove the effectiveness of our model, we propose a new FashionEdit dataset that simulates the real-world clothing design workflow, evaluated from generation similarity, user satisfaction, and quality. The code and dataset: https://github.com/detectiveli/FashionEdit."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09324","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:31:17Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:31:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:31:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09310","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09310","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09310","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Javanmardi","name":"Javanmardi, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manabu","familyName":"Tsukada","name":"Tsukada, Manabu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"You Share Beliefs, I Adapt: Progressive Heterogeneous Collaborative Perception"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:53:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:26:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Collaborative perception enables vehicles to overcome individual perception limitations by sharing information, allowing them to see further and through occlusions. In real-world scenarios, models on different vehicles are often heterogeneous due to manufacturer variations. Existing methods for heterogeneous collaborative perception address this challenge by fine-tuning adapters or the entire network to bridge the domain gap. However, these methods are impractical in real-world applications, as each new collaborator must undergo joint training with the ego vehicle on a dataset before inference, or the ego vehicle stores models for all potential collaborators in advance. Therefore, we pose a new question: Can we tackle this challenge directly during inference, eliminating the need for joint training? To answer this, we introduce Progressive Heterogeneous Collaborative Perception (PHCP), a novel framework that formulates the problem as few-shot unsupervised domain adaptation. Unlike previous work, PHCP dynamically aligns features by self-training an adapter during inference, eliminating the need for labeled data and joint training. Extensive experiments on the OPV2V dataset demonstrate that PHCP achieves strong performance across diverse heterogeneous scenarios. Notably, PHCP achieves performance comparable to SOTA methods trained on the entire dataset while using only a small amount of unlabeled data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09310","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:58Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09307","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09307","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09307","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengzhao","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Zhengzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youbin","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Youbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haonan","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Haonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinpu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jinpu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongqing","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Hongqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benyou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Benyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Multimodal LLMs See Materials Clearly? A Multimodal Benchmark on Materials Characterization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:50:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:26:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Materials characterization is fundamental to acquiring materials information, revealing the processing-microstructure-property relationships that guide material design and optimization. While multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have recently shown promise in generative and predictive tasks within materials science, their capacity to understand real-world characterization imaging data remains underexplored. To bridge this gap, we present MatCha, the first benchmark for materials characterization image understanding, comprising 1,500 questions that demand expert-level domain expertise. MatCha encompasses four key stages of materials research comprising 21 distinct tasks, each designed to reflect authentic challenges faced by materials scientists. Our evaluation of state-of-the-art MLLMs on MatCha reveals a significant performance gap compared to human experts. These models exhibit degradation when addressing questions requiring higher-level expertise and sophisticated visual perception. Simple few-shot and chain-of-thought prompting struggle to alleviate these limitations. These findings highlight that existing MLLMs still exhibit limited adaptability to real-world materials characterization scenarios. We hope MatCha will facilitate future research in areas such as new material discovery and autonomous scientific agents. MatCha is available at https://github.com/FreedomIntelligence/MatCha."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09307","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:54Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09290","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09290","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09290","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony P.","familyName":"Addison","name":"Addison, Anthony P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wentian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natalie","familyName":"Voets","name":"Voets, Natalie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Kamnitsas","name":"Kamnitsas, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modality-Agnostic Input Channels Enable Segmentation of Brain lesions in Multimodal MRI with Sequences Unavailable During Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:25:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:25:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Segmentation models are important tools for the detection and analysis of lesions in brain MRI. Depending on the type of brain pathology that is imaged, MRI scanners can acquire multiple, different image modalities (contrasts). Most segmentation models for multimodal brain MRI are restricted to fixed modalities and cannot effectively process new ones at inference. Some models generalize to unseen modalities but may lose discriminative modality-specific information. This work aims to develop a model that can perform inference on data that contain image modalities unseen during training, previously seen modalities, and heterogeneous combinations of both, thus allowing a user to utilize any available imaging modalities. We demonstrate this is possible with a simple, thus practical alteration to the U-net architecture, by integrating a modality-agnostic input channel or pathway, alongside modality-specific input channels. To train this modality-agnostic component, we develop an image augmentation scheme that synthesizes artificial MRI modalities. Augmentations differentially alter the appearance of pathological and healthy brain tissue to create artificial contrasts between them while maintaining realistic anatomical integrity. We evaluate the method using 8 MRI databases that include 5 types of pathologies (stroke, tumours, traumatic brain injury, multiple sclerosis and white matter hyperintensities) and 8 modalities (T1, T1+contrast, T2, PD, SWI, DWI, ADC and FLAIR). The results demonstrate that the approach preserves the ability to effectively process MRI modalities encountered during training, while being able to process new, unseen modalities to improve its segmentation. Project code: https://github.com/Anthony-P-Addison/AGN-MOD-SEG"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to MICCAI 2025, for the following workshop: ML-CDS 2025: Multimodal Learning and Fusion Across Scales for Clinical Decision Support"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09290","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:30Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09286","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09286","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09286","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Bohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiadi","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jiadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Chern","name":"Chern, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhulin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ya","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Programmability: A Guide for Code-as-Thought in Chart Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:22:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:25:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chart understanding presents a critical test to the reasoning capabilities of Vision-Language Models (VLMs). Prior approaches face critical limitations: some rely on external tools, making them brittle and constrained by a predefined toolkit, while others fine-tune specialist models that often adopt a single reasoning strategy, such as text-based chain-of-thought (CoT). The intermediate steps of text-based reasoning are difficult to verify, which complicates the use of reinforcement-learning signals that reward factual accuracy. To address this, we propose a Code-as-Thought (CaT) approach to represent the visual information of a chart in a verifiable, symbolic format. Our key insight is that this strategy must be adaptive: a fixed, code-only implementation consistently fails on complex charts where symbolic representation is unsuitable. This finding leads us to introduce Visual Programmability: a learnable property that determines if a chart-question pair is better solved with code or direct visual analysis. We implement this concept in an adaptive framework where a VLM learns to choose between the CaT pathway and a direct visual reasoning pathway. The selection policy of the model is trained with reinforcement learning using a novel dual-reward system. This system combines a data-accuracy reward to ground the model in facts and prevent numerical hallucination, with a decision reward that teaches the model when to use each strategy, preventing it from defaulting to a single reasoning mode. Experiments demonstrate strong and robust performance across diverse chart-understanding benchmarks. Our work shows that VLMs can be taught not only to reason but also how to reason, dynamically selecting the optimal reasoning pathway for each task."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09286","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:25Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09263","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09263","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09263","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yehui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zach","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Zach","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DATE: Dynamic Absolute Time Enhancement for Long Video Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:49:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:23:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long video understanding remains a fundamental challenge for multimodal large language models (MLLMs), particularly in tasks requiring precise temporal reasoning and event localization. Existing approaches typically adopt uniform frame sampling and rely on implicit position encodings to model temporal order. However, these methods struggle with long-range dependencies, leading to critical information loss and degraded temporal comprehension. In this paper, we propose Dynamic Absolute Time Enhancement (DATE) that enhances temporal awareness in MLLMs through the Timestamp Injection Mechanism (TIM) and a semantically guided Temporal-Aware Similarity Sampling (TASS) strategy. Specifically, we interleave video frame embeddings with textual timestamp tokens to construct a continuous temporal reference system. We further reformulate the video sampling problem as a vision-language retrieval task and introduce a two-stage algorithm to ensure both semantic relevance and temporal coverage: enriching each query into a descriptive caption to better align with the vision feature, and sampling key event with a similarity-driven temporally regularized greedy strategy. Our method achieves remarkable improvements w.r.t. absolute time understanding and key event localization, resulting in state-of-the-art performance among 7B and 72B models on hour-long video benchmarks. Particularly, our 7B model even exceeds many 72B models on some benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09263","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:49Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:29:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09254","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09254","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09254","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanpeng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yanpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Kaixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhuo","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Lizhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinrong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jinrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi Yong H.","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Qi Yong H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lun M.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Lun M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuo Feng","familyName":"Hung","name":"Hung, Kuo Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Better Dental AI: A Multimodal Benchmark and Instruction Dataset for Panoramic X-ray Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:39:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:22:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large vision-language models (LVLMs) have demonstrated strong performance on general-purpose medical tasks. However, their effectiveness in specialized domains such as dentistry remains underexplored. In particular, panoramic X-rays, a widely used imaging modality in oral radiology, pose interpretative challenges due to dense anatomical structures and subtle pathological cues, which are not captured by existing medical benchmarks or instruction datasets. To this end, we introduce MMOral, the first large-scale multimodal instruction dataset and benchmark tailored for panoramic X-ray interpretation. MMOral consists of 20,563 annotated images paired with 1.3 million instruction-following instances across diverse task types, including attribute extraction, report generation, visual question answering, and image-grounded dialogue. In addition, we present MMOral-Bench, a comprehensive evaluation suite covering five key diagnostic dimensions in dentistry. We evaluate 64 LVLMs on MMOral-Bench and find that even the best-performing model, i.e., GPT-4o, only achieves 41.45% accuracy, revealing significant limitations of current models in this domain. To promote the progress of this specific domain, we also propose OralGPT, which conducts supervised fine-tuning (SFT) upon Qwen2.5-VL-7B with our meticulously curated MMOral instruction dataset. Remarkably, a single epoch of SFT yields substantial performance enhancements for LVLMs, e.g., OralGPT demonstrates a 24.73% improvement. Both MMOral and OralGPT hold significant potential as a critical foundation for intelligent dentistry and enable more clinically impactful multimodal AI systems in the dental field. The dataset, model, benchmark, and evaluation suite are available at https://github.com/isbrycee/OralGPT."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"40 pages, 26 figures, 9 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09254","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:36Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:29:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanwei","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoning","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Haoning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zicheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peilin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Peilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris Wei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Chris Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linhan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Linhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xiangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixia","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weixia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dandan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Dandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangtao","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Guangtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiongkuo","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Xiongkuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhichao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyue","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xinyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Shubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anh","familyName":"Dao","name":"Dao, Anh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Hongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaojiao","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Jiaojiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiding","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yiding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yupeng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiran","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Feiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijuan","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Lijuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VQualA 2025 Challenge on Visual Quality Comparison for Large Multimodal Models: Methods and Results"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:00:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:19:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a summary of the VQualA 2025 Challenge on Visual Quality Comparison for Large Multimodal Models (LMMs), hosted as part of the ICCV 2025 Workshop on Visual Quality Assessment. The challenge aims to evaluate and enhance the ability of state-of-the-art LMMs to perform open-ended and detailed reasoning about visual quality differences across multiple images. To this end, the competition introduces a novel benchmark comprising thousands of coarse-to-fine grained visual quality comparison tasks, spanning single images, pairs, and multi-image groups. Each task requires models to provide accurate quality judgments. The competition emphasizes holistic evaluation protocols, including 2AFC-based binary preference and multi-choice questions (MCQs). Around 100 participants submitted entries, with five models demonstrating the emerging capabilities of instruction-tuned LMMs on quality assessment. This challenge marks a significant step toward open-domain visual quality reasoning and comparison and serves as a catalyst for future research on interpretable and human-aligned quality evaluation systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICCV VQualA Workshop 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:04Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:28:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09172","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09172","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09172","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chunxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaoxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meiling","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Meiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boming","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Boming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunjian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yunjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyang","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Xiangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging the Gap Between Ideal and Real-world Evaluation: Benchmarking AI-Generated Image Detection in Challenging Scenarios"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T06:15:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:18:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of generative models, highly realistic image synthesis has posed new challenges to digital security and media credibility. Although AI-generated image detection methods have partially addressed these concerns, a substantial research gap remains in evaluating their performance under complex real-world conditions. This paper introduces the Real-World Robustness Dataset (RRDataset) for comprehensive evaluation of detection models across three dimensions: 1) Scenario Generalization: RRDataset encompasses high-quality images from seven major scenarios (War and Conflict, Disasters and Accidents, Political and Social Events, Medical and Public Health, Culture and Religion, Labor and Production, and everyday life), addressing existing dataset gaps from a content perspective. 2) Internet Transmission Robustness: examining detector performance on images that have undergone multiple rounds of sharing across various social media platforms. 3) Re-digitization Robustness: assessing model effectiveness on images altered through four distinct re-digitization methods. We benchmarked 17 detectors and 10 vision-language models (VLMs) on RRDataset and conducted a large-scale human study involving 192 participants to investigate human few-shot learning capabilities in detecting AI-generated images. The benchmarking results reveal the limitations of current AI detection methods under real-world conditions and underscore the importance of drawing on human adaptability to develop more robust detection algorithms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICCV2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09172","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:39Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:27:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09154","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09154","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09154","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bui Duc","familyName":"Manh","name":"Manh, Bui Duc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soumyaratna","familyName":"Debnath","name":"Debnath, Soumyaratna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zetong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zetong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shriram","familyName":"Damodaran","name":"Damodaran, Shriram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arvind","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Arvind","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yueyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Mi","name":"Mi, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik","familyName":"Cambria","name":"Cambria, Erik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mind Meets Space: Rethinking Agentic Spatial Intelligence from a Neuroscience-inspired Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T05:23:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:16:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in agentic AI have led to systems capable of autonomous task execution and language-based reasoning, yet their spatial reasoning abilities remain limited and underexplored, largely constrained to symbolic and sequential processing. In contrast, human spatial intelligence, rooted in integrated multisensory perception, spatial memory, and cognitive maps, enables flexible, context-aware decision-making in unstructured environments. Therefore, bridging this gap is critical for advancing Agentic Spatial Intelligence toward better interaction with the physical 3D world. To this end, we first start from scrutinizing the spatial neural models as studied in computational neuroscience, and accordingly introduce a novel computational framework grounded in neuroscience principles. This framework maps core biological functions to six essential computation modules: bio-inspired multimodal sensing, multi-sensory integration, egocentric-allocentric conversion, an artificial cognitive map, spatial memory, and spatial reasoning. Together, these modules form a perspective landscape for agentic spatial reasoning capability across both virtual and physical environments. On top, we conduct a framework-guided analysis of recent methods, evaluating their relevance to each module and identifying critical gaps that hinder the development of more neuroscience-grounded spatial reasoning modules. We further examine emerging benchmarks and datasets and explore potential application domains ranging from virtual to embodied systems, such as robotics. Finally, we outline potential research directions, emphasizing the promising roadmap that can generalize spatial reasoning across dynamic or unstructured environments. We hope this work will benefit the research community with a neuroscience-grounded perspective and a structured pathway. Our project page can be found at Github."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"54 pages, journal"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09154","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:11Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:27:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09118","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09118","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09118","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianlu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Tianlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"An","name":"An, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Ziyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaicheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qichuan","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Qichuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gradient-Attention Guided Dual-Masking Synergetic Framework for Robust Text-based Person Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T03:06:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:13:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) exhibits strong performance across diverse vision tasks, its application to person representation learning faces two critical challenges: (i) the scarcity of large-scale annotated vision-language data focused on person-centric images, and (ii) the inherent limitations of global contrastive learning, which struggles to maintain discriminative local features crucial for fine-grained matching while remaining vulnerable to noisy text tokens. This work advances CLIP for person representation learning through synergistic improvements in data curation and model architecture. First, we develop a noise-resistant data construction pipeline that leverages the in-context learning capabilities of MLLMs to automatically filter and caption web-sourced images. This yields WebPerson, a large-scale dataset of 5M high-quality person-centric image-text pairs. Second, we introduce the GA-DMS (Gradient-Attention Guided Dual-Masking Synergetic) framework, which improves cross-modal alignment by adaptively masking noisy textual tokens based on the gradient-attention similarity score. Additionally, we incorporate masked token prediction objectives that compel the model to predict informative text tokens, enhancing fine-grained semantic representation learning. Extensive experiments show that GA-DMS achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP2025 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09118","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:21Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:26:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09114","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09114","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09114","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kelin","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Kelin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chan-Yang","familyName":"Ju","name":"Ju, Chan-Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong-Ho","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dong-Ho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modality Alignment with Multi-scale Bilateral Attention for Multimodal Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T02:52:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:12:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746252.3761222","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal recommendation systems are increasingly becoming foundational technologies for e-commerce and content platforms, enabling personalized services by jointly modeling users' historical behaviors and the multimodal features of items (e.g., visual and textual). However, most existing methods rely on either static fusion strategies or graph-based local interaction modeling, facing two critical limitations: (1) insufficient ability to model fine-grained cross-modal associations, leading to suboptimal fusion quality; and (2) a lack of global distribution-level consistency, causing representational bias. To address these, we propose MambaRec, a novel framework that integrates local feature alignment and global distribution regularization via attention-guided learning. At its core, we introduce the Dilated Refinement Attention Module (DREAM), which uses multi-scale dilated convolutions with channel-wise and spatial attention to align fine-grained semantic patterns between visual and textual modalities. This module captures hierarchical relationships and context-aware associations, improving cross-modal semantic modeling. Additionally, we apply Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) and contrastive loss functions to constrain global modality alignment, enhancing semantic consistency. This dual regularization reduces mode-specific deviations and boosts robustness. To improve scalability, MambaRec employs a dimensionality reduction strategy to lower the computational cost of high-dimensional multimodal features. Extensive experiments on real-world e-commerce datasets show that MambaRec outperforms existing methods in fusion quality, generalization, and efficiency. Our code has been made publicly available at https://github.com/rkl71/MambaRec."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CIKM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09114","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:15Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:26:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09090","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09090","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09090","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyu","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Hengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yijiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanrui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Huanrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SQAP-VLA: A Synergistic Quantization-Aware Pruning Framework for High-Performance Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T01:52:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:11:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models exhibit unprecedented capabilities for embodied intelligence. However, their extensive computational and memory costs hinder their practical deployment. Existing VLA compression and acceleration approaches conduct quantization or token pruning in an ad-hoc manner but fail to enable both for a holistic efficiency improvement due to an observed incompatibility. This work introduces SQAP-VLA, the first structured, training-free VLA inference acceleration framework that simultaneously enables state-of-the-art quantization and token pruning. We overcome the incompatibility by co-designing the quantization and token pruning pipeline, where we propose new quantization-aware token pruning criteria that work on an aggressively quantized model while improving the quantizer design to enhance pruning effectiveness. When applied to standard VLA models, SQAP-VLA yields significant gains in computational efficiency and inference speed while successfully preserving core model performance, achieving a $\\times$1.93 speedup and up to a 4.5\\% average success rate enhancement compared to the original model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09090","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:38Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:25:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09064","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09064","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09064","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuhui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qiuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuancheng","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Xuancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huping","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Huping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing 3D Medical Image Understanding with Pretraining Aided by 2D Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T00:12:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:09:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding 3D medical image volumes is critical in the medical field, yet existing 3D medical convolution and transformer-based self-supervised learning (SSL) methods often lack deep semantic comprehension. Recent advancements in multimodal large language models (MLLMs) provide a promising approach to enhance image understanding through text descriptions. To leverage these 2D MLLMs for improved 3D medical image understanding, we propose Med3DInsight, a novel pretraining framework that integrates 3D image encoders with 2D MLLMs via a specially designed plane-slice-aware transformer module. Additionally, our model employs a partial optimal transport based alignment, demonstrating greater tolerance to noise introduced by potential noises in LLM-generated content. Med3DInsight introduces a new paradigm for scalable multimodal 3D medical representation learning without requiring human annotations. Extensive experiments demonstrate our state-of-the-art performance on two downstream tasks, i.e., segmentation and classification, across various public datasets with CT and MRI modalities, outperforming current SSL methods. Med3DInsight can be seamlessly integrated into existing 3D medical image understanding networks, potentially enhancing their performance. Our source code, generated datasets, and pre-trained models will be available at https://github.com/Qybc/Med3DInsight."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IEEE Journal of Biomedical and Health Informatics (JBHI)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09064","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:02Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:25:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09013","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09013","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09013","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monjoy Narayan","familyName":"Choudhury","name":"Choudhury, Monjoy Narayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junling","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mrinmaya","familyName":"Sachan","name":"Sachan, Mrinmaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Vision-Language Models Solve Visual Math Equations?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T21:16:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:06:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite strong performance in visual understanding and language-based reasoning, Vision-Language Models (VLMs) struggle with tasks requiring integrated perception and symbolic computation. We study this limitation through visual equation solving, where mathematical equations are embedded in images, variables are represented by object icons, and coefficients must be inferred by counting. While VLMs perform well on textual equations, they fail on visually grounded counterparts. To understand this gap, we decompose the task into coefficient counting and variable recognition, and find that counting is the primary bottleneck, even when recognition is accurate. We also observe that composing recognition and reasoning introduces additional errors, highlighting challenges in multi-step visual reasoning. Finally, as equation complexity increases, symbolic reasoning itself becomes a limiting factor. These findings reveal key weaknesses in current VLMs and point toward future improvements in visually grounded mathematical reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Monjoy Narayan Choudhury and Junling Wang contributed equally to this work. Accepted at EMNLP2025 main. Code and datasets are open-sourced with links in the paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09013","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:23:51Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:23:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:23:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08940","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08940","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08940","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lisa","familyName":"Dunlap","name":"Dunlap, Lisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph E.","familyName":"Gonzalez","name":"Gonzalez, Joseph E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trevor","familyName":"Darrell","name":"Darrell, Trevor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian Caba","familyName":"Heilbron","name":"Heilbron, Fabian Caba","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josef","familyName":"Sivic","name":"Sivic, Josef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Russell","name":"Russell, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discovering Divergent Representations between Text-to-Image Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T19:07:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:03:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we investigate when and how visual representations learned by two different generative models diverge. Given two text-to-image models, our goal is to discover visual attributes that appear in images generated by one model but not the other, along with the types of prompts that trigger these attribute differences. For example, \"flames\" might appear in one model's outputs when given prompts expressing strong emotions, while the other model does not produce this attribute given the same prompts. We introduce CompCon (Comparing Concepts), an evolutionary search algorithm that discovers visual attributes more prevalent in one model's output than the other, and uncovers the prompt concepts linked to these visual differences. To evaluate CompCon's ability to find diverging representations, we create an automated data generation pipeline to produce ID2, a dataset of 60 input-dependent differences, and compare our approach to several LLM- and VLM-powered baselines. Finally, we use CompCon to compare popular text-to-image models, finding divergent representations such as how PixArt depicts prompts mentioning loneliness with wet streets and Stable Diffusion 3.5 depicts African American people in media professions. Code at: https://github.com/adobe-research/CompCon"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICCV 2025. Code available at https://github.com/adobe-research/CompCon"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08940","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:22:09Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:22:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:22:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08935","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08935","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08935","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad","familyName":"Alberb","name":"Alberb, Muhammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helen","familyName":"Cheung","name":"Cheung, Helen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anne","familyName":"Martel","name":"Martel, Anne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Live(r) Die: Predicting Survival in Colorectal Liver Metastasis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T19:02:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:03:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Colorectal cancer frequently metastasizes to the liver, significantly reducing long-term survival. While surgical resection is the only potentially curative treatment for colorectal liver metastasis (CRLM), patient outcomes vary widely depending on tumor characteristics along with clinical and genomic factors. Current prognostic models, often based on limited clinical or molecular features, lack sufficient predictive power, especially in multifocal CRLM cases. We present a fully automated framework for surgical outcome prediction from pre- and post-contrast MRI acquired before surgery. Our framework consists of a segmentation pipeline and a radiomics pipeline. The segmentation pipeline learns to segment the liver, tumors, and spleen from partially annotated data by leveraging promptable foundation models to complete missing labels. Also, we propose SAMONAI, a novel zero-shot 3D prompt propagation algorithm that leverages the Segment Anything Model to segment 3D regions of interest from a single point prompt, significantly improving our segmentation pipeline's accuracy and efficiency. The predicted pre- and post-contrast segmentations are then fed into our radiomics pipeline, which extracts features from each tumor and predicts survival using SurvAMINN, a novel autoencoder-based multiple instance neural network for survival analysis. SurvAMINN jointly learns dimensionality reduction and hazard prediction from right-censored survival data, focusing on the most aggressive tumors. Extensive evaluation on an institutional dataset comprising 227 patients demonstrates that our framework surpasses existing clinical and genomic biomarkers, delivering a C-index improvement exceeding 10%. Our results demonstrate the potential of integrating automated segmentation algorithms and radiomics-based survival analysis to deliver accurate, annotation-efficient, and interpretable outcome prediction in CRLM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Thesis at Erasmus Mundus Joint Master's Degree in Medical Imaging and Applications"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08935","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:22:03Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:22:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:22:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08913","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08913","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08913","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sin-Yu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Sin-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent W. S.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Vincent W. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:18:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:01:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semantic communication has shown outstanding performance in preserving the overall source information in wireless transmission. For semantically rich content such as images, human users are often interested in specific regions depending on their intent. Moreover, recent semantic coding models are mostly trained on specific datasets. However, real-world applications may involve images out of the distribution of training dataset, which makes generalization a crucial but largely unexplored problem. To incorporate user's intent into semantic coding, in this paper, we propose a generalized user-oriented image semantic coding (UO-ISC) framework, where the user provides a text query indicating its intent. The transmitter extracts features from the source image which are relevant to the user's query. The receiver reconstructs an image based on those features. To enhance the generalization ability, we integrate contrastive language image pre-training (CLIP) model, which is a pretrained large vision-language model (VLM), into our proposed UO-ISC framework. To evaluate the relevance between the reconstructed image and the user's query, we introduce the user-intent relevance loss, which is computed by using a pretrained large VLM, large language-and-vision assistant (LLaVA) model. When performing zero-shot inference on unseen objects, simulation results show that the proposed UO-ISC framework outperforms the state-of-the-art query-aware image semantic coding in terms of the answer match rate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IEEE Global Communications Conference (GLOBECOM), Taipei, Taiwan, Dec. 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08913","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:21:32Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:21:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:21:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08829","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08829","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08829","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chandan Kumar","familyName":"Sah","name":"Sah, Chandan Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PerFairX: Is There a Balance Between Fairness and Personality in Large Language Model Recommendations?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-20T09:41:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into recommender systems has enabled zero-shot, personality-based personalization through prompt-based interactions, offering a new paradigm for user-centric recommendations. However, incorporating user personality traits via the OCEAN model highlights a critical tension between achieving psychological alignment and ensuring demographic fairness. To address this, we propose PerFairX, a unified evaluation framework designed to quantify the trade-offs between personalization and demographic equity in LLM-generated recommendations. Using neutral and personality-sensitive prompts across diverse user profiles, we benchmark two state-of-the-art LLMs, ChatGPT and DeepSeek, on movie (MovieLens 10M) and music (Last.fm 360K) datasets. Our results reveal that personality-aware prompting significantly improves alignment with individual traits but can exacerbate fairness disparities across demographic groups. Specifically, DeepSeek achieves stronger psychological fit but exhibits higher sensitivity to prompt variations, while ChatGPT delivers stable yet less personalized outputs. PerFairX provides a principled benchmark to guide the development of LLM-based recommender systems that are both equitable and psychologically informed, contributing to the creation of inclusive, user-centric AI applications in continual learning contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures. Accepted to the Workshop on Multimodal Continual Learning (MCL) at ICCV 2025. @2025 IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision Workshops (ICCVW), ICCV's 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08829","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:19:34Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:19:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:19:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":41,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=%28titles.title%3A%28%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+%22vision-language+models%22+OR+%22vision-language-action%22+OR+%22vision-language-action+models%22+OR+multimodal+OR+VLA+OR+VLAs+OR+VLM+OR+VLMs+OR+MLLM+OR+MLLMs%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+%22vision-language+models%22+OR+%22vision-language-action%22+OR+%22vision-language-action+models%22+OR+multimodal+OR+VLA+OR+VLAs+OR+VLM+OR+VLMs+OR+MLLM+OR+MLLMs%29%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28representation+OR+representations+OR+generation+OR+diffusion+OR+flow+OR+action+OR+actions+OR+training+OR+fusion+OR+evaluation+OR+reasoning+OR+navigation%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28representation+OR+representations+OR+generation+OR+diffusion+OR+flow+OR+action+OR+actions+OR+training+OR+fusion+OR+evaluation+OR+reasoning+OR+navigation%29%29+AND+registered%3A%5B2025-09-11T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-12T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026sort=registered"}}